<body>

<header class="site-header">
  <div class="eyebrow">SUNY Morrisville · Faculty Professional Development · May 19, 2026</div>
  <h1>AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Steven M. Schneider · SUNY Polytechnic Institute · AIX Center</div>
</header>

<nav class="tab-nav">
  <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="showTab('intro')">Intro</button>
  <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showTab('talk')">Talk</button>
  <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showTab('resources')">Resources</button>
</nav>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
     TAB 1: INTRO
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div id="tab-intro" class="tab-panel active">
  <div class="label">Session Overview</div>
  <h2>What You're Looking At</h2>
  <p>This page is the talk. Not a slide deck — a document. It was produced in conversation with an AI, and that process is part of what I want to show you today.</p>
  <p>Before I make an argument about AI literacy, I want to show you what AI literacy looks like in practice — as a working method, not a topic.</p>

  <div class="demo-block">
    <div class="demo-label">Live Demo · GitHub</div>
    <p>Everything I'm showing you today lives at a public GitHub repository. The talk text, the drafts that preceded it, the conversation archives that produced the drafts, and a README that tells you what each file is and why it's there.</p>
    <p>This is what a transparent AI workflow looks like. The artifact is the page. The work is the archive.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>The Learner's Permit</h3>
  <p>The first thing I built for my students is a 15-exercise scaffold I call the Learner's Permit. It takes about five hours. It produces a set of conversation transcripts, a synthesis document, and a credential.</p>
  <p>The credential is not for completing the exercises. It is for demonstrating, in the transcripts, that you know what you're doing.</p>

  <div class="question-block">
    <div class="q">Can I turn this in for credit?</div>
    <p>This is the question every faculty member needs an answer to before Fall 2026. The answer is not "yes" or "no." It is a framework — and I want to show you the one I use.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>How This Page Was Made</h3>
  <p>The talk you're about to hear was developed across five drafts. Each draft emerged from a conversation with Claude. The conversation transcripts are archived. The page was built from those drafts.</p>
  <p>If you want to make something like this — a talk, a course module, an assignment sequence — the method is the same. Prompt. Read. Revise. Archive. The transcript is the work. The page is evidence the work concluded.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:2rem; color: var(--muted); font-size:0.85rem; font-style:italic;">The talk begins on the next tab.</p>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
     TAB 2: TALK
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div id="tab-talk" class="tab-panel">
  <div class="label">The Talk · v5 Canonical</div>
  <h2>AI Literacy in Applied Contexts:<br><em>When the AI Tells You What to Do</em></h2>

  <div class="distinction-grid">
    <div class="distinction-card compositional">
      <div class="card-label">The case pedagogy was built for</div>
      <h4>Compositional</h4>
      <p>Student prompts a model. Model returns text. Student revises, argues, edits. The transcript is evidence of thinking. The document is the product.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="distinction-card directive">
      <div class="card-label">The case your students face</div>
      <h4>Directive</h4>
      <p>AI is embedded infrastructure. It has already recommended an action before the student begins evaluating. The demand is judgment, not revision.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="talk-section">
    <h3>Opening</h3>
    <p>When most people in higher education talk about AI literacy, they mean something like this: a student prompts a model, gets text back, and makes decisions about what to do with that output. The evidence of their thinking is in the conversation. The product is a document, an argument, a piece of writing.</p>
    <p>That is the compositional case. And almost all of the AI literacy pedagogy being developed right now — the frameworks, the rubrics, the course guidelines — was built for that case.</p>
    <p>I want to walk you around campus for a few minutes, because I don't think that describes your students.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="talk-section">
    <div class="stop-marker">Stop One</div>
    <h3>The Dairy Farm</h3>
    <p>Your students working in automated dairy operations are going to encounter a milking parlor that looks like this.</p>
    <p>Robotic milking systems handle the milking. The cows come and go on their own schedule. The AI monitors each animal: milk yield, conductivity, milking frequency, body weight, reproductive status. The system makes feeding recommendations. It flags animals for attention. It surfaces patterns that suggest a cow needs follow-up.</p>
    <p>Your student's job is not to milk the cow. It's to read the dashboard, evaluate the flag, and decide whether to act on it.</p>
    <p>The system has already shaped the field of attention. Your student arrives after the dashboard has decided what looks important.</p>
    <p>And that term — <em>attention</em> — is not accidental.</p>
    <p>The architecture underneath many modern AI systems is literally called a transformer, built around what computer scientists call an attention mechanism: a way of weighting which signals matter most in a given context. Humans do something similar. We constantly prioritize attention through training, instinct, experience, and judgment. But now another attention system is entering the workflow before the human operator begins evaluating the situation.</p>
    <p><strong>The dashboard is not the situation. It is the model's interpretation of the situation.</strong></p>
    <p>Under what circumstances should your student override it? That's not a technical question. That's a literacy question.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="talk-section">
    <div class="stop-marker">Stop Two</div>
    <h3>The Auto Shop</h3>
    <p>Your automotive technology students are going to work in shops where the first thing that happens when a vehicle comes in is someone plugs in an OBD-II scanner.</p>
    <p>The scanner reads the diagnostic trouble codes. AI-assisted diagnostic platforms cross-reference those codes against repair records for similar vehicles, group related codes, prioritize probable causes, and present a recommended repair sequence with confidence scores.</p>
    <p>Your student sees the recommendation on a tablet.</p>
    <p>The AI has already organized what looks important. Your student's attention is directed before their hands-on inspection begins.</p>
    <p>They might run additional tests. They might catch something the scanner missed. But the workflow is: recommendation first, verification second — if at all.</p>
    <p>What does it take to recognize when the recommendation is wrong? That's not a scanning skill. That's a judgment skill.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="talk-section">
    <div class="stop-marker">Stop Three</div>
    <h3>The Greenhouse</h3>
    <p>Your horticulture students are going to manage crops in environments where AI is already making autonomous decisions.</p>
    <p>Irrigation systems adjust water delivery based on soil moisture sensors and weather data — without asking anyone. Climate control systems set temperature, humidity, and CO₂ levels. Camera systems scan for disease and pest presence, flag anomalies, and recommend treatments.</p>
    <p>The grower — your student — receives a report. An alert. A dashboard.</p>
    <p>The system has often already acted before they see it.</p>
    <p>What does fixation look like when the disease alert fires and attention goes to the flagged plant while the actual outbreak is two rows over? What does it cost when the dashboard declares the field healthy and your student stops looking?</p>
    <p>In each of these three cases, the cognitive challenge is the same: the AI system increasingly organizes what the operator notices first.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="talk-section">
    <h3>The Cognitive Challenge</h3>
    <p>Human-factors researchers have documented recurring failure modes when operators work alongside automated systems. You see them in aviation, in clinical decision support, in process control:</p>
    <div class="failure-modes">
      <div class="failure-mode-item">Manual skill atrophy</div>
      <div class="failure-mode-item">Plan continuation bias</div>
      <div class="failure-mode-item">Vigilance decrement</div>
      <div class="failure-mode-item">Omission errors</div>
      <div class="failure-mode-item">Commission errors</div>
      <div class="failure-mode-item">Confirmation bias</div>
      <div class="failure-mode-item">Expectation-driven processing</div>
    </div>
    <p>What these have in common is not complexity. They are all failures of attention — the operator's attention has been pre-organized by the system, and independent judgment has been displaced by cue-following.</p>
    <p>Aviation addressed several of these through mandatory manual flying and scenario-based training under realistic conditions. The FAA can mandate that because the stakes are existential.</p>
    <p>Your students at Morrisville are not pilots. We do not need to train them to do everything manually as a backup. But we do need to train them to maintain an independent model of the situation — to recognize when their own observation of the cow, the car, or the plant contradicts what the system is telling them, and to know what to do with that contradiction.</p>
    <p>That is a practiced habit of judgment. It does not emerge from instruction about AI.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="talk-section">
    <h3>What Makes This Round Different</h3>
    <p>Many earlier automation failures were more mechanically legible. The pump breaks. The sensor malfunctions. You can see it. You can trace it.</p>
    <p>This round of AI-assisted automation is different in three ways.</p>
    <p>First, the logic is opaque. The system makes a recommendation based on patterns it learned from large amounts of data. You cannot always ask it why and get a clear answer.</p>
    <p>Second, it fails by being confidently wrong. AI can produce a plausible-looking recommendation that happens to be incorrect for your specific cow, your specific vehicle, your specific crop — and nothing in the interface signals that.</p>
    <p>Third, introducing AI into automation may actually increase the cognitive demand on the human operator. The milking robot reduced physical labor. The AI-assisted milking system requires your student to constantly evaluate recommendations from a system they cannot fully see into, at scale, every day.</p>
    <p>The issue is not simply that AI produces recommendations. The issue is that AI systems increasingly organize what the operator notices first — before the operator has had a chance to form an independent assessment.</p>

    <div class="key-sentence">
      The question is not whether students can use AI. It is whether they can maintain an independent model of the situation while AI is already organizing what they see.
    </div>

    <p>AI literacy at Morrisville isn't simpler than AI literacy at a liberal arts college. It may be harder.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="talk-section">
    <h3>What AI Literacy Means Here</h3>
    <p><strong>Not:</strong> teach students to use the tools.</p>
    <p><strong>Not:</strong> teach students about automation bias.</p>
    <p><strong>This:</strong> design courses where students practice the moment of judgment before compliance. Where the AI recommendation is sometimes wrong by design. Where they have to articulate why they're pausing. Where following the system blindly costs them something in simulation before it costs them something in the field.</p>
    <p>Students need to learn to recognize the dashboard as model-generated rather than reality-generated. That is a different habit than spotting hallucinations in a text. It is the habit of maintaining epistemic separation between what the model surfaced and what is actually happening in the barn, the shop, or the greenhouse.</p>
    <p>Your dairy students should encounter scenarios where the feeding algorithm has miscalculated and they have to catch it by reading the herd. Your auto students should encounter vehicles where the scanner recommendation is wrong and their hands-on inspection is the only thing that catches it. Your horticulture students should encounter disease alerts that are false positives — and silent outbreaks the system missed.</p>
    <p>That's not a new course. That's a design principle you can embed in the courses you're already teaching.</p>
    <p>AI literacy in applied fields is not primarily about producing content with AI. It is about preserving independent professional judgment while AI systems increasingly organize what appears important before humans even begin to think.</p>
    <p><strong>That is what AI literacy looks like at SUNY Morrisville.</strong></p>
  </div>
</div>

