          <!-- 0. TITLE + DISCLOSURE -->
          <section>
            <span class="label">SUNY Morrisville Faculty Development · May 19, 2026</span>
            <h1>AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</h1>
            <h3>When the AI Tells You What to Do</h3>
            <div class="confession">
              I want to tell you something before we start.<br><br>
              One hundred percent of this content is AI-generated — except my prompts.<br><br>
              I had background knowledge of Morrisville and AI literacy before I started. I learned the details — the aviation literature, the failure modes, the greenhouse systems — through sustained conversation with a language model.<br><br>
              I'll come back to that at the end.
            </div>
            <p class="mono">Steven M. Schneider · Professor of Information Design &amp; Technology · Co-Director, AIX Center</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 1. THE COMPOSITIONAL ASSUMPTION -->
          <section>
            <span class="label">The Problem</span>
            <h2>Most AI Literacy Was Built for the Wrong Student</h2>
            <p>When higher education talks about AI literacy, it means this: a student prompts a model, gets text back, and makes decisions about what to do with that output. The evidence of their thinking is in the conversation. The product is a document.</p>
            <p>That is the <strong>compositional case.</strong></p>
            <p>Almost all of the AI literacy pedagogy being developed right now — the frameworks, the rubrics, the course guidelines — was built for that case.</p>
            <p>I want to walk you around campus, because I don't think that describes your students.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 2. DAIRY FARM -->
          <section>
            <div class="stop-header">Stop One · The Dairy Farm</div>
            <h2>The Dashboard Has Already Decided What Looks Important</h2>
            <p>Robotic milking systems handle the milking. The AI monitors each animal: milk yield, conductivity, milking frequency, body weight, reproductive status. It makes feeding recommendations. It flags animals for attention.</p>
            <div class="dashboard">▸ Animal #47 — yield ↓ 12% — flag: possible mastitis — recommended action: isolate + veterinary review</div>
            <p>Your student's job is not to milk the cow. It's to read the dashboard, evaluate the flag, and decide whether to act on it.</p>
            <p><em>The system has already shaped the field of attention. Your student arrives after the dashboard has decided what looks important.</em></p>
          </section>

          <!-- 3. ATTENTION / TRANSFORMER DETOUR -->
          <section>
            <span class="label">The Concept</span>
            <h2>The Dashboard Is Not the Situation</h2>
            <p>That word — <strong>attention</strong> — is not accidental.</p>
            <p>The architecture underneath many modern AI systems is literally called a <strong>transformer</strong>, built around what computer scientists call an <em>attention mechanism</em>: a way of weighting which signals matter most in a given context.</p>
            <p>Humans do something similar. We constantly prioritize attention through training, instinct, experience, and judgment.</p>
            <p>But now another attention system is entering the workflow <em>before</em> the human operator begins evaluating the situation.</p>
            <p><strong>The dashboard is not the situation. It is the model's interpretation of the situation.</strong></p>
            <p>Under what circumstances should your student override it? That is not a technical question. That is a literacy question.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 4. AUTO SHOP -->
          <section>
            <div class="stop-header">Stop Two · The Auto Shop</div>
            <h2>The Recommendation Is the Starting Point</h2>
            <p>The first thing that happens when a vehicle comes in: someone plugs in an OBD-II scanner.</p>
            <p>AI-assisted diagnostic platforms cross-reference trouble codes against repair records for similar vehicles, group related codes, prioritize probable causes, and present a recommended repair sequence with confidence scores.</p>
            <div class="dashboard">▸ P0300 — Random/Multiple Cylinder Misfire — probable cause: ignition coil pack (confidence: 87%) — recommended: replace coil pack cylinders 2, 4</div>
            <p>Your student sees the recommendation on a tablet.</p>
            <p><em>The AI has already organized what looks important. Your student's attention is directed before their hands-on inspection begins.</em></p>
            <p>What does it take to recognize when the recommendation is wrong? That is not a scanning skill. That is a judgment skill.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 5. GREENHOUSE -->
          <section>
            <div class="stop-header">Stop Three · The Greenhouse</div>
            <h2>The System Has Often Already Acted</h2>
            <p>Irrigation systems adjust water delivery automatically. Climate control sets temperature, humidity, and CO₂ levels. Camera systems scan for disease, flag anomalies, recommend treatments.</p>
            <div class="dashboard">▸ Bay 3 — botrytis risk: HIGH — sensor confidence: 94% — recommended: fungicide application within 24h</div>
            <p>Your student receives a report. An alert. A dashboard. The system has often already acted before they see it.</p>
            <p>What does fixation look like when the disease alert fires and attention goes to the flagged plant while the actual outbreak is two rows over?</p>
            <p>In each of these three cases, the cognitive challenge is the same: <strong>the AI system increasingly organizes what the operator notices first.</strong></p>
          </section>

          <!-- 6. SEVEN FAILURE MODES -->
          <section>
            <span class="label">The Evidence</span>
            <h2>Human-Factors Researchers Have Documented This</h2>
            <p>The same failure modes appear in aviation, clinical decision support, and process control when operators work alongside automated systems:</p>
            <div class="failure-grid">
              <div class="failure-item"><span class="failure-num">01</span><span><strong>Manual skill atrophy</strong> — procedural skills degrade from disuse</span></div>
              <div class="failure-item"><span class="failure-num">02</span><span><strong>Plan continuation bias</strong> — cognitive commitment to the AI-established course despite new cues</span></div>
              <div class="failure-item"><span class="failure-num">03</span><span><strong>Vigilance decrement</strong> — attention drifts from primary indicators during automation</span></div>
              <div class="failure-item"><span class="failure-num">04</span><span><strong>Omission errors</strong> — failing to notice when the system misses something</span></div>
              <div class="failure-item"><span class="failure-num">05</span><span><strong>Commission errors</strong> — acting on AI recommendations without independent verification</span></div>
              <div class="failure-item"><span class="failure-num">06</span><span><strong>Confirmation bias</strong> — misinterpreting ambiguous information to confirm the AI's recommendation</span></div>
              <div class="failure-item"><span class="failure-num">07</span><span><strong>Expectation-driven processing</strong> — seeing what the AI predicted, not what is actually there</span></div>
            </div>
            <p>What these share: the operator's attention has been pre-organized by the system. Independent judgment has been displaced by cue-following.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 7. WHAT MAKES THIS ROUND DIFFERENT -->
          <section>
            <span class="label">The Distinction</span>
            <h2>What Makes This Round of Automation Different</h2>
            <p>Many earlier automation failures were more mechanically legible. The pump breaks. The sensor malfunctions. You can see it.</p>
            <p>This round is different in three ways:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>The logic is opaque.</strong> The system makes a recommendation based on patterns learned from large amounts of data. You cannot always ask it why.</li>
              <li><strong>It fails by being confidently wrong.</strong> AI produces plausible-looking recommendations that happen to be incorrect for your specific cow, vehicle, or crop — and nothing in the interface signals that.</li>
              <li><strong>It may increase cognitive demand.</strong> The milking robot reduced physical labor. The AI-assisted milking system requires your student to evaluate recommendations from a system they cannot fully see into, at scale, every day.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>The question is not whether students can use AI. It is whether they can maintain an independent model of the situation while AI is already organizing what they see.</strong></p>
          </section>

          <!-- 8. COMPOSITIONAL VS DIRECTIVE TABLE -->
          <section>
            <span class="label">The Framework</span>
            <h2>Two Different Cognitive Demands</h2>
            <table class="compare">
              <thead><tr><th></th><th>Compositional</th><th>Directive</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Human role</strong></td><td>Author / editor</td><td>Decision-maker / operator</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>AI output</strong></td><td>Text to shape</td><td>Recommendation to act on</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Primary risk</strong></td><td>Delegation — accepting the first draft</td><td>Automation bias — acting without verification</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Literacy requirement</strong></td><td>Revision and critical evaluation</td><td>Situated judgment before action</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Training environment</strong></td><td>Transcript review and rubric</td><td>Scenario simulation with stakes</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>The AI literacy literature has developed primarily for the compositional case. The pedagogical frameworks for directive contexts are largely unbuilt.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 9. WHAT AI LITERACY MEANS HERE -->
          <section>
            <span class="label">The Pedagogy</span>
            <h2>What AI Literacy Looks Like at SUNY Morrisville</h2>
            <p>Not: teach students to use the tools. Not: teach students about automation bias.</p>
            <p><strong>This:</strong> design courses where students practice the moment of judgment before compliance.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>The AI recommendation is sometimes wrong — <em>deliberately, by design.</em></li>
              <li>Students have to articulate why they are pausing.</li>
              <li>Following the system blindly costs them something in simulation before it costs them something in the field.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Students need to learn to recognize the dashboard as model-generated rather than reality-generated. That is different from spotting hallucinations in a text. It is the habit of maintaining epistemic separation between what the model surfaced and what is actually happening in the barn, the shop, or the greenhouse.</p>
            <p>That is not a new course. That is a design principle you can embed in the courses you are already teaching.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 10. CLOSING: CAN I GET CREDIT -->
          <section>
            <span class="label">Closing</span>
            <h2>AI Literacy in Applied Fields</h2>
            <p>AI literacy in applied fields is not primarily about producing content with AI.</p>
            <p>It is about <strong>preserving independent professional judgment</strong> while AI systems increasingly organize what appears important before humans even begin to think.</p>
            <p>That is the challenge. The simulation tier is the design response. And you can start building it tomorrow, inside courses you are already teaching.</p>
            <hr style="border:none;border-top:1px solid #ddd;margin:1.5em 0;">
            <p>I said at the start that 100% of this content is AI-generated — except my prompts. The full transcript of the conversations that produced this talk is in the GitHub repository I am sharing with you today. You can zip it, upload it to a language model, and ask it questions.</p>
